<item>
            <title>[LU-20883] ptlrpc: GSS error reply to a resent RPC uses the old match bits and is dropped</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20883</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Problem&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since &lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-14594&quot; title=&quot;reply to the original RPC can land to the resent RPC reply buffer&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-14594&quot;&gt;&lt;del&gt;LU-14594&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (OBD_CONNECT2_REP_MBITS, 2.14.52), a resent RPC gets new reply match bits. The sender waits for the reply on the new bits (&lt;tt&gt;ptl_send_rpc()&lt;/tt&gt;, niobuf.c). The receiver reads the new bits from the request into &lt;tt&gt;rq_rep_mbits&lt;/tt&gt; and uses them for the reply (&lt;tt&gt;ptlrpc_server_handle_req_in()&lt;/tt&gt;, service.c).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But when the GSS layer rejects the request, &lt;tt&gt;ptlrpc_server_handle_req_in()&lt;/tt&gt; sends the error reply as soon as &lt;tt&gt;sptlrpc_svc_unwrap_request()&lt;/tt&gt; returns &lt;tt&gt;SECSVC_COMPLETE&lt;/tt&gt;. That is before &lt;tt&gt;rq_rep_mbits&lt;/tt&gt; is set. So the error reply uses &lt;tt&gt;rq_xid&lt;/tt&gt; as match bits. For a resend these are the old bits. LNet on the sender finds no match and drops the reply. The sender then waits a full request timeout, instead of seeing the GSS error at once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first send is not affected, because its match bits are equal to its XID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Where it was seen&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;sanity-sec test_27d and test_27e, review-dne-selinux-ssk-part-2 (el8.10), Gerrit 67783 patch set 6 (&lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20542&quot; title=&quot;ptlrpc/gss: server evicts client when its reverse GSS context goes stale, instead of re-establishing it&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-20542&quot;&gt;LU-20542&lt;/a&gt;). There an OSS resends an LDLM completion AST over a reverse context that the client has already dropped.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OSS debug log (27d):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;preformatted panel&quot; style=&quot;border-width: 1px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;preformattedContent panelContent&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;ptlrpc_set_mbits()) resend with new mbits old x1878292362931072 new x1878292362931200
lnet_parse_put()) Dropping PUT from 12345-10.240.23.83@tcp portal 16 match 1878292362931072 offset 360 length 320: 4
ptlrpc_expire_one_request()) @@@ Request sent has timed out for slow reply: [sent 1791279480/real 1791279480] req@ff4b3aab007f56c0 x1878292362931072/t0(0) o105-&amp;gt;lustre-OST0003@10.240.23.83@tcp:15/16 ...
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The client debug log shows that it got the resend and rejected it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;preformatted panel&quot; style=&quot;border-width: 1px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;preformattedContent panelContent&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;ptlrpc_server_handle_req_in()) unwrap req x1878292362931072
gss_svc_searchbyctx()) ctx hdl 0xb6b53fed0af81423 does not have mech ctx: rc = -2
ptlrpc_server_handle_req_in()) finish req x1878292362931072
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;27e shows the same sequence on OST0004. Logs: &lt;a href=&quot;https://testing.whamcloud.com/test_sets/13406571-a9fa-4d90-ae90-819a9acf44be&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;https://testing.whamcloud.com/test_sets/13406571-a9fa-4d90-ae90-819a9acf44be&lt;/a&gt; (the per-subtest &lt;tt&gt;debug_log&lt;/tt&gt; files).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Scope&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same receive path handles forward RPCs. So a client resend that hits a GSS error on the server should lose the error reply in the same way. This is not tested.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Possible fixes&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;On the receiver: send the error reply on the request&apos;s match bits. This only works if the receiver can read them. With the &lt;tt&gt;skpi&lt;/tt&gt; flavor the request body is encrypted, and a rejected request cannot be decrypted. So this does not cover every case.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;On the sender: keep the old match bits for a resend that can only get a GSS error. &lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20542&quot; title=&quot;ptlrpc/gss: server evicts client when its reverse GSS context goes stale, instead of re-establishing it&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-20542&quot;&gt;LU-20542&lt;/a&gt; (Gerrit 67783) does this for its own reverse-context resend. In general the sender does not know in advance that a GSS error will come back.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;A general fix needs a decision on which side should change.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
